text:
katie: Heads up — hitting an intermittent hang when downloading large streamed responses from the Redwood API using our .NET client. Happens under load after a few retries. Anyone seen this?

sam: Oof, description? code sample?

katie: minimal repro:
```csharp
using var client = new HttpClient();
var resp = await client.GetAsync(url, HttpCompletionOption.ResponseContentRead);
resp.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
await using var stream = await resp.Content.ReadAsStreamAsync();
await stream.CopyToAsync(fileStream);
```
We wrap the whole call in a Polly retry policy. Works fine usually but eventually it just stalls and never completes.

lee: Two quick suspects: 1) HttpClient lifetime (creating per request) -> socket exhaustion. 2) Response buffering vs ResponseHeadersRead + how Polly retries interact with a streamed body.

matt: ^^ agree. Also, if Polly retries the Send and the first response's stream wasn't disposed, the server or SocketsHttpHandler might run out of connections. Are you using HttpClientFactory or new HttpClient() per request?

katie: currently new HttpClient() in a helper; we weren't aware of HttpCompletionOption nuance.

sam: Try these changes:
- Use IHttpClientFactory / AddHttpClient in DI.
- Use HttpCompletionOption.ResponseHeadersRead when you intend to stream and then manually read the stream. That avoids buffering the whole body.
- Don't wrap the entire stream-reading block inside a retry policy. Only retry the Send/Receive (i.e., the initial SendAsync) or network errors.

lee: Example pattern we use:
```csharp
// registration
services.AddHttpClient("redwood").ConfigurePrimaryHttpMessageHandler(() => new SocketsHttpHandler { PooledConnectionLifetime = TimeSpan.FromMinutes(10), MaxConnectionsPerServer = 100 });
// usage
var client = httpClientFactory.CreateClient("redwood");
var req = new HttpRequestMessage(HttpMethod.Get, url);
// policy executes the SendAsync only
await retryPolicy.ExecuteAsync(async ct => {
    var resp = await client.SendAsync(req, HttpCompletionOption.ResponseHeadersRead, ct);
    resp.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
    await using var stream = await resp.Content.ReadAsStreamAsync(ct);
    await stream.CopyToAsync(destinationStream, 81920, ct);
}, CancellationToken.None);
```
Note: we call SendAsync inside the policy but then immediately stream the response. If the policy retries, make sure previous response got disposed. In this pattern the resp is disposed when leaving the lambda for that attempt.

matt: Also consider limiting what triggers a retry. Polly.Handle<HttpRequestException>() or HandleResult<HttpResponseMessage>(r => r.StatusCode == HttpStatusCode.ServiceUnavailable) — avoid retrying on exceptions thrown while reading the stream (IOException/TaskCanceled) unless you expect the server to support range requests and resume.

katie: That makes sense. We were doing EnsureSuccessStatusCode after streaming which meant some failures happened mid-stream and Polly retried but the socket stayed tied up.

sam: Another detail: use ResponseHeadersRead and set a read timeout (cancellation token + TimeoutPolicy) so hung connections are cancelled and returned to pool. Avoid Task.Wait on async code — causes threadpool starvation under load.

lee: If you can't change server to resume, then don't retry large-body downloads automatically. Instead, stream into a temp file and validate checksum; if failed then re-download from scratch with a fresh request + client.

katie: Will switch to IHttpClientFactory and move retry to only the SendAsync. Also will set MaxConnectionsPerServer and add a timeout policy.

matt: One more nit: when using AddHttpClient with Polly via .AddPolicyHandler(...) the policy wraps the entire SendAsync call, but by default the handler pipeline disposes the response when the request attempt finishes only if you don't hold onto the response outside the delegate. Keep the streaming inside the same attempt as sanely scoped.

katie: Thanks — trying this now.

ci-bot: deploy-status:pr-checks all green for branch/streaming-retries-fix :white_check_mark: (ci: 1765324750)

katie: quick update — change to ResponseHeadersRead + IHttpClientFactory + retry on Send only fixed the hang in local stress tests. Will roll to staging.

sam: cheers :tada: Ping if staging shows anything odd and we can tweak PooledConnectionLifetime.

lee: FYI, if you still see leaks, inspect NETSTAT for TIME_WAIT/ESTABLISHED counts and make sure SocketsHttpHandler settings match your expected connection churn.

katie: will do, thanks all.
